  urldate = {2026-07-14},
  abstract = {People record their everyday experiences with pictures and videos, and they are frequently considered reliable sources of information. The reliance on picture editing tools, digital photography technologies, and social media has given rise to new issues that demand the attention of academics, business executives, and security systems, particularly with regard to the identification and blocking of fake multimedia content on social media. The capacity to control fake images and videos has become more complex because of the swift advancement in generative AI models and picture editing tools. The act of manipulating multimedia content, such as images and videos, for malevolent purposes is becoming more commonplace over time. This can have detrimental effects on the psychological, physical, political, and financial well-being of those targeted, as well as their close friends and families. The study presents a comparative analysis of different methods for detecting image forgeries for detecting manipulated and AI-generated images, and discusses the most commonly used benchmark datasets. Additionally, the goal of the study is to shed light on how various deep learning techniques function and their performance. Also, we will discuss image forgery types (manipulated images and AI-generated images). Manipulated images include splicing and copy-move forgery, while generated images include those produced by Diffusion Models (DM), Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs), and Variational Autoencoders (VAEs). Finally, we will discuss the limitations and the research directions.},
  keywords = {/unread,Deep learning,DeepFake,Forgery,Generative adversarial networks,Generative Adversarial Networks,Image Forensics,Image Forgery Detection,Manuals,Reliability,Reviews,Security,Social networking (online),Splicing,Streaming media},
