  abstract = {Recently, deepfake technology has made its way into the medical field due to a string of state-of-the-art AI algorithms. These include Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs), Variational Autoencoders (VAEs), and diffusion models. Although synthetic medical images provide opportunities for data augmentation, anonymization, and medical training, they also pose serious risks, including insurance fraud, diagnostic errors, and ethical challenges. This study surveys state-of-the-art techniques for the creation and detection of deepfake medical images. This review highlights common generative models, their applications in healthcare, and their inherent risks. Furthermore, active and passive detection approaches have been analyzed, ranging from watermarking techniques to deep learning and frequency-domain forensics. Emerging directions, including blockchain integration, quantum-assisted detection, and explainable AI, were also discussed. Finally, this paper emphasizes the need for regulatory frameworks, multimodal authentication, and human-in-the-loop systems to ensure trustworthy medical imaging in the era of AI.},
