  abstract = {The development of social media platforms is one of the greatest advancements in technology as it has reached billions around the globe. With such technology, image sharing has increased exponentially, making digital imaging an important point of communication. Unfortunately, an equal concern regarding social media image sharing has emerged that is the alteration of digital images. In turn, this current dangerous risks to the information integrity, an individual's privacy, and even the trust of the public when trying to authenticate the reality. The purpose of this survey study is to provide an overview of the recent developments in the detection of the digital tampering of social media images using AI (artificial intelligence) algorithms. It explains about different categories of tampering such as splicing, copy-move, retouching, and deepfake. And also analyzes the potential of machine learning, deep learning, and hybrid models on detecting these changes. This paper also analyzes social media, including datasets that the public uses, performance evaluation metrics, benchmark models, and social media-specific challenges unique to the environment like compression artifacts, low resolution, and even adversarial image manipulation. It concludes with gaps in the existing literature and offers methods on enhancing robustness, detection speed, and generalization across the social media services for future needs.},
