  abstract = {Recently, both privacy and encryption for end-users have turned out to be vital aspects of Social Media (SM), secure systems, and platforms. Nevertheless, this coexistence introduces various technical and governance obstacles for both privacy and encryption. This review paper seeks to explore the balance between privacy and encryption of end-users within SM platforms, focusing on End-To-End Encryption (E2EE), information protection, and digital privacy. While E2EE is developed to ensure confidentiality and protect customers from unauthorized access, it concurrently limits visibility for content material moderation, chance detection, and digital forensics. The study also analyzes the recent protection frameworks, privacy-enhancing technologies, and regulatory impacts to focus on how systems attempt to stabilize robust encryption with accountability and end-user protection. However, the key challenges include scalability, the misuse of encrypted channels for spreading misinformation, and ensuring deepfake compliance with data protection regulations. By integrating the latest research developments, this study points out the importance of privacy-preserving security mechanisms that maintain trust, protect personal data, and support platform functionality. The findings render our contributions to ongoing discussions on designing steady, privacy-preserving SM systems in an increasing number of regulated digital surroundings.},
